26.4 Firestore rules — deployed
The rules have been rewritten and deployed. Current state:

orders — read is open (see note below), create is false,
update is admin-only plus a scoped driver path (driver can only move
their own assigned order through collected → on_the_way → delivered,
and can only touch the specific fields those transitions write),
delete is admin-only

pendingOrders — full deny for clients. Only Cloud Functions write here.

users — owner can read and write their own doc, but cannot
change walletBalance, paidEarnings, totalEarnings, or role.
Wallet-zeroing is only permitted as part of the account soft-delete
flow (deleted: true + walletBalance: 0 in the same write).

drivers — read is admin or the driver themselves. Driver update
cannot change paidEarnings, totalEarnings, approved, suspended,
rating, or reviewCount. Admin is unconstrained.

refundRequests — customer can create their own with
status: 'pending'; admin can also create on behalf. Update is admin
only, and only for the fields message, userPhone, userName,
updatedAt — status transitions are server-only via the
rejectRefund and markRefundPaid callables.

notifications — read is open to any signed-in user (this fixes
the customer bell). Update is admin plus readBy / deletedFor for
self.

Server-managed collections — full deny for clients:
idempotencyKeys, walletReservations, refundOperations,
creditNoteOperations, orderAcceptOperations,
processedWebhookEvents. unmatchedWebhookEvents is admin-read-only.

⚠️ Note on the orders read rule: to make the store-accept page
work (see §26.6), orders/{orderId} read was opened to
if isSignedIn() for the session. This means any signed-in customer
can technically read any order by its ID. Order IDs are Firestore
random 20-char strings, so guessing one is impractical, but this is
weaker than intended and is a known compromise. If the store-accept
page is later moved to a Cloud Function that reads orders via Admin SDK,
this rule can be closed back to "owner, assigned driver, admin".

