27.5 The [hidden] CSS override hazard — read this
This cost us hours. Understanding it will save time in the future.

The browser's default stylesheet has:

css
[hidden] { display: none; }
Any class-level display rule beats this. Example:

css
.store-hours-grid {
  display: grid;   /* ← specific selector, beats [hidden] */
}
If .store-hours-grid also has the hidden attribute, the browser shows it
anyway — the class rule wins. Setting element.hidden = true in JS has no
visual effect.

Whenever you write a class that sets display and the element is also toggled
with hidden, you MUST add a matching rule:

css
.store-hours-grid[hidden] {
  display: none;
}
The [hidden] attribute selector combined with the class selector has higher
specificity than the class alone, so display: none wins when hidden is
present.

Where to check: search base.css and store.css for any class where the
element is also toggled with hidden or [hidden]. If there's no matching
[hidden] { display: none } rule, it's a latent bug.

