29. Update — 3 November 2026 (Session Handover #9)
Read §0 (DO NOT CHANGE) first. Then §21 through §28. This section captures a massive debugging session focused on reducing Firestore reads, fixing mobile caching bugs, and resolving timezone issues. The site is deployed and stable.

29.1 What was done in this session
Massive Read Reduction & Caching (catalog-cache.js)

The Problem: The site was burning through Firestore reads by fetching the entire 500+ product catalog on the homepage, store page, and cart page every single time a user visited.

The Solution: We created public/js/catalog-cache.js. This module stores the 500+ products and all stores in localStorage.

How it works: On page load, it checks the settings/catalog version document in Firestore (1 read). If the version matches the local cache, it serves the 500+ products from the phone's memory (0 reads). If the admin changes a product, the version increments, and the cache refreshes on the next load.

Result: Store pages and Cart pages now load instantly and cost near-zero reads for returning users.

The Homepage Specials Revert (No more caching for Specials)

The Problem: Caching specials caused a nightmare of bugs. Specials expired at midnight, but the cache kept showing them until the version bumped. The timezone hacks we applied to fix this caused dates to shift backwards every time the admin clicked "Update".

The Solution: We completely removed getSpecialsCache() from the homepage and stores page.

How it works: The Homepage and Stores page now query Firestore directly for active specials on every load (limit 100). This guarantees 100% accuracy for expiration dates.

The Trade-off: It costs ~100 reads per homepage visit, but stability was prioritized over absolute read-minimization for specials.

Admin Date Shift Bug (Fixed)

The Problem: When editing a special in admin/js/products.js, clicking "Update" caused the start date and end date to shift backwards by one day, every time.

The Cause: JavaScript's toISOString() was converting the date to UTC, which pushed it back a day.

The Fix: We now save dates as plain strings ("2026-11-03") instead of Date objects. We also removed the toISOString() conversion. The dates are now completely locked in place.

Product Modal Refactor (Mobile Bug Fix)

The Problem: On mobile PWAs, navigating away from the homepage and coming back caused the "Other Specials" rail to disappear because the mobile browser wiped the memory.

The Fix: We removed the homepage rail entirely. We also updated the modal to detect if it is on a Store Page (using isStandaloneStorePage()).

Result:

Homepage: Shows item, price, "Visit Store", and "Add to Cart".

Store Page: Shows item, price, "Add to Cart" (hides Visit Store), and shows the category rail using memory.

29.2 DO NOT CHANGE (additions to §0)
Do NOT re-enable caching for Specials. The getSpecialsCache() function is dead. We fetch specials directly from Firestore on every load to guarantee expired items vanish at midnight.

Do NOT revert toDateInput() in admin/js/products.js. It uses getFullYear(), getMonth(), and getDate() to read local dates. If you change this back to toISOString(), the dates will shift backwards again.

Do NOT change how specialStartDate and specialEndDate are saved in admin/js/products.js. They are saved as plain strings ("2026-11-03"), not Date objects. This prevents timezone math.

Do NOT remove isStandaloneStorePage() from product-modal.js. It is what hides the "Visit Store" button when the user is already inside a store.

Do NOT remove bumpCatalogVersion() from the Admin files. This is what automatically tells the catalog cache to refresh when you add/edit products.

